ng cricket or acting. They decide by themselves that they’re “undeserving” of music, sport, art and drama. Unbelievably, in 2019, children as young as 10 are already hard-wired with the self- limiting poverty notion of “not for the likes of us”. This is heartbreaking. It’s hardly news that life is tougher for poorer children, but it’s an outrage if all sense of curiosity, artistry and playfulness is knocked out of them so early. Something is wrong if better-off children feel entitled to explore and participate in areas that interest and excite them, while the disadvantaged are cast from the start as cultural wallﬂowers – doomed to sit out every dance. This isn’t about every child learning the piano or violin (but why not?), it’s about sowing the seeds for a cultural hinterland that will sustain and enrich them for life. It’s about people exploring their passions and refusing to have their horizons artiﬁcially limited. Regardless of circumstances, all children should know that their faces “ﬁt” – automatically and forever. Culture belongs to everyone. SubQuestion No : 198",C,Children will have feelings of low self-esteem and self-exclusion.,Official SSC CGL 2018 Tier 2 response sheet highlights Option C (Children will have feelings of low self-esteem and self-exclusion.) as the official answer under English Grammar & Vocabulary.,Hard,Verified,Verified (Official Response Sheet Key),2018 Tier 2 (2019-09-11 Shift 3 (Evening)),https://sscportal.in/sites/default/files/download-cgl-tier-2-papers-english-language-and-comprehension-11-sep-2019-evening-shift.pdf
