SSC-CGL-2021-T1-QA-EN-11313,SSC CGL,2021,Tier 1,2022-04-20,Shift 1,Quantitative Aptitude,Algebra,English,"If (0.4x + 1/x) = 5, what is the value of (0.064x 3 + 1/x 3 )?",105,110,119,125,C,119,"Let a = 0.4x and b = 1/x. Then a + b = 5 and ab = 0.4. a³ + b³ = (a + b)³ − 3ab(a + b) = 125 − 3 × 0.4 × 5 = 125 − 6 = 119, and a³ = 0.064x³. Answer: 119",Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2021 Tier 1 (2022-04-20 Shift 1),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2021-2022-04-20-s1
SSC-CGL-2021-T1-QA-EN-11314,SSC CGL,2021,Tier 1,2022-04-20,Shift 1,Quantitative Aptitude,Geometry,English,"A triangle with the lengths of its sides proportional to the numbers 7, 24 and 30 is:",acute angled,right angled,obtuse angled,not possible,C,obtuse angled,"Take sides 7k, 24k, 30k. Compare the square of the longest side with the sum of the other two squares: 7² + 24² = 49 + 576 = 625, while 30² = 900. Since 900 > 625 (and 7 + 24 > 30, so the triangle exists), the triangle is obtuse angled. Answer: obtuse angled",Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2021 Tier 1 (2022-04-20 Shift 1),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2021-2022-04-20-s1
SSC-CGL-2021-T1-QA-EN-11315,SSC CGL,2021,Tier 1,2022-04-20,Shift 1,Quantitative Aptitude,Algebra,English,"If x 2 + 1/x 2 = 18, x > 0, then find the value of x 3 + 1/x 3 .",17√5,46√5,52,34√5,D,34√5,"(x + 1/x)² = x² + 1/x² + 2 = 20, so x + 1/x = 2√5 (x > 0). x³ + 1/x³ = (x + 1/x)³ − 3(x + 1/x) = 40√5 − 6√5 = 34√5. Answer: 34√5",Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2021 Tier 1 (2022-04-20 Shift 1),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2021-2022-04-20-s1
SSC-CGL-2021-T1-QA-EN-11316,SSC CGL,2021,Tier 1,2022-04-20,Shift 1,Quantitative Aptitude,Percentage,English,"The given bar chart represents the number of Televisions Sets (TV) manufactured (in thousands) and the respective percentage of those TV Sets sold by five different companies A, B, C, D and E in 2015. Study the chart carefully and answer the question that follows. (Data from the official bar chart, shown as a table.) Company TV Sets manufactured (in thousands) % of TV Sets sold A 64 75 B 90 64 C 70 82 D 85 56 E 58 77 The average number of TV sets sold by companies C and D is what percentage of the number of TV sets manufactured by company E? Express your answer correct to one place of decimal.",86.5%,92.2%,90.5%,89.1%,C,90.5%,Sold by C = 82% of 70 = 57.4 thousand; sold by D = 56% of 85 = 47.6 thousand. Average = (57.4 + 47.6) ÷ 2 = 52.5 thousand. As a percentage of E’s production: 52.5 ÷ 58 × 100 ≈ 90.5%. Answer: 90.5%,Hard,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2021 Tier 1 (2022-04-20 Shift 1),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2021-2022-04-20-s1
SSC-CGL-2021-T1-QA-EN-11317,SSC CGL,2021,Tier 1,2022-04-20,Shift 1,Quantitative Aptitude,Trigonometry & Heights,English,Ram travelled from a place Z to P at an average speed of 130 km/h. He travelled the first 75% of the distance in two-third of the time and the rest at an average speed of X km/h. The value of X/2 is:,48.75,51,19.25,97.5,A,48.75,"Let the whole journey take time T, so the distance is 130T. The last 25% of the distance, 32.5T, is covered in the remaining one-third of the time, T/3. X = 32.5T ÷ (T/3) = 97.5 km/h, so X/2 = 48.75. Answer: 48.75",Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2021 Tier 1 (2022-04-20 Shift 1),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2021-2022-04-20-s1
SSC-CGL-2021-T1-QA-EN-11318,SSC CGL,2021,Tier 1,2022-04-20,Shift 1,Quantitative Aptitude,Trigonometry & Heights,English,"In a circle with centre O, PA and PB are tangents to the circle at point A and point B, respectively. C is a point on the major arc AB. If ∠ACB = 50°, then find the measure of ∠APB.",90°,80°,100°,50°,B,80°,"∠AOB at the centre = 2 × ∠ACB = 100°. In quadrilateral PAOB the angles at A and B are 90° (radius ⊥ tangent), so ∠APB = 180° − 100° = 80°. Answer: 80°",Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2021 Tier 1 (2022-04-20 Shift 1),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2021-2022-04-20-s1
SSC-CGL-2021-T1-QA-EN-11319,SSC CGL,2021,Tier 1,2022-04-20,Shift 1,Quantitative Aptitude,Trigonometry & Heights,English,"The average of eleven consecutive positive integers is d . If the last two numbers are excluded, by how much will the average increase or decrease?",Will increase by 1,Will decrease by 2,Will increase by 2,Will decrease by 1,D,Will decrease by 1,"For consecutive integers the average is the middle term: of 11 numbers it is the 6th number, d. Removing the last two leaves the first 9, whose average is the 5th number, d − 1. So the average decreases by 1. Answer: Will decrease by 1",Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2021 Tier 1 (2022-04-20 Shift 1),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2021-2022-04-20-s1
SSC-CGL-2021-T1-QA-EN-11320,SSC CGL,2021,Tier 1,2022-04-20,Shift 1,Quantitative Aptitude,Percentage,English,"Study the given bar graph and answer the question that follows. The bar graph shows the exports of cars of type A and B (in ₹ millions) from 2014 to 2018. (Data from the official bar graph, shown as a table.) Year A (₹ millions) B (₹ millions) 2014 200 225 2015 150 250 2016 275 200 2017 175 275 2018 300 325 The total exports of cars of type A from 2014 to 2016 is approximately what percentage less than the total exports of cars of type B from 2015 to 2017 (correct to one decimal place)?",11.7%,13.8%,10.4%,11.3%,B,13.8%,"Type A, 2014–2016: 200 + 150 + 275 = 625. Type B, 2015–2017: 250 + 200 + 275 = 725 (₹ millions). 625 is (725 − 625)/725 × 100 ≈ 13.8% less than 725. Answer: 13.8%",Hard,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2021 Tier 1 (2022-04-20 Shift 1),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2021-2022-04-20-s1
SSC-CGL-2021-T1-QA-EN-11321,SSC CGL,2021,Tier 1,2022-04-20,Shift 1,Quantitative Aptitude,Trigonometry & Heights,English,Simplify the following expression: cosec 4 A(1 − cos 4 A) − 2cot 2 A − 1,1,cosec²A,sin²A,0,D,0,"1 − cos⁴A = (1 − cos²A)(1 + cos²A) = sin²A(1 + cos²A), so cosec⁴A(1 − cos⁴A) = cosec²A(1 + cos²A) = cosec²A + cot²A = 1 + 2cot²A. Then 1 + 2cot²A − 2cot²A − 1 = 0. Answer: 0",Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2021 Tier 1 (2022-04-20 Shift 1),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2021-2022-04-20-s1
SSC-CGL-2021-T1-QA-EN-11322,SSC CGL,2021,Tier 1,2022-04-20,Shift 1,Quantitative Aptitude,Trigonometry & Heights,English,"Manjeet bought a second-hand motorbike for ₹22,000 and spent ₹3,000 on its overhauling and maintenance. He then sold it with 12% profit. If he had sold it for ₹500 less, then what would have been his profit percentage?",10%,8%,5%,10.5%,A,10%,"Total cost = 22,000 + 3,000 = ₹25,000; selling price at 12% profit = ₹28,000. Selling for ₹500 less gives ₹27,500, a profit of 2,500/25,000 × 100 = 10%. Answer: 10%",Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2021 Tier 1 (2022-04-20 Shift 1),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2021-2022-04-20-s1
SSC-CGL-2021-T1-QA-EN-11323,SSC CGL,2021,Tier 1,2022-04-20,Shift 1,Quantitative Aptitude,Simple & Compound Interest,English,Find the value of k in the number 3426k if the number is divisible by 6 but NOT divisible by 5.,3,6,9,4,B,6,"Divisible by 6 means divisible by 2 and 3; not divisible by 5 means k ≠ 0 (and k is even, so k ≠ 5). Digit sum 3 + 4 + 2 + 6 + k = 15 + k must be a multiple of 3, so even k ∈ {0, 6}; excluding 0 gives k = 6. Answer: 6",Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2021 Tier 1 (2022-04-20 Shift 1),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2021-2022-04-20-s1
SSC-CGL-2021-T1-QA-EN-11324,SSC CGL,2021,Tier 1,2022-04-20,Shift 1,Quantitative Aptitude,Trigonometry & Heights,English,"If 6 tanA (tanA + 1) = 5 − tanA, Given that 0 < A < π/2 what is the value of (sinA + cosA)?",5√3,5/√3,3√5,3/√5,D,3/√5,"Let t = tan A: 6t² + 6t = 5 − t → 6t² + 7t − 5 = 0 → (2t − 1)(3t + 5) = 0, so t = 1/2 (A is acute). Then sin A = 1/√5 and cos A = 2/√5, so sin A + cos A = 3/√5. Answer: 3/√5",Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2021 Tier 1 (2022-04-20 Shift 1),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2021-2022-04-20-s1
