SSC-CGL-2022-T1-GI-EN-11960,SSC CGL,2022,Tier 1,2022-12-01,Shift 1,General Intelligence & Reasoning,Coding & Decoding,English,"In a certain code language, FRUCTUS is coded as 108, and SPRINTER is coded as 119. How will MASCULINE be coded in that language?",99,97,96,98,B,97,The code is the sum of the alphabet positions: FRUCTUS = 6 + 18 + 21 + 3 + 20 + 21 + 19 = 108; SPRINTER = 19 + 16 + 18 + 9 + 14 + 20 + 5 + 18 = 119. MASCULINE = 13 + 1 + 19 + 3 + 21 + 12 + 9 + 14 + 5 = 97. Answer: 97,Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2022 Tier 1 (2022-12-01 Shift 1),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2022-12-01-s1
SSC-CGL-2022-T1-GI-EN-11961,SSC CGL,2022,Tier 1,2022-12-01,Shift 1,General Intelligence & Reasoning,Logical Reasoning,English,"Select the odd group of numbers. (NOTE: Operations should be performed on the whole numbers, without breaking down the numbers into its constituent digits. E.g.13 – Operations on 13 such as adding /subtracting /multiplying etc. to 13 can be performed. Breaking down 13 into 1 and 3 and then performing mathematical operations on 1 and 3 is not allowed)",(6 – 216 – 206),(5 – 125 – 115),(7 – 343 – 331),(4 – 64 – 54),C,(7 – 343 – 331),"In each group the second number is the cube of the first and the third is 10 less: 216 − 10 = 206, 125 − 10 = 115, 64 − 10 = 54. For 7, 343 − 12 = 331, so (7 – 343 – 331) is the odd group. Answer: (7 – 343 – 331)",Hard,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2022 Tier 1 (2022-12-01 Shift 1),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2022-12-01-s1
SSC-CGL-2022-T1-GI-EN-11962,SSC CGL,2022,Tier 1,2022-12-01,Shift 1,General Intelligence & Reasoning,Syllogism & Statements,English,In the following question below are given some statements followed by some conclusions based on those statements. Taking the given statements to be true even if they seem to be at variance from commonly known facts. Read all the conclusions and then decide which of the given conclusion logically follows the given statements. Statements: I. All W are S. II. No D is W. Conclusion: I. Some S are W. II. All S are D. III. No W is D.,All conclusion follows,Only conclusion III follows,Both conclusions I and III follows,Both conclusions I and II follows,C,Both conclusions I and III follows,"All W are S, so some S are W: conclusion I follows. No D is W is the same as no W is D: conclusion III follows. Nothing says all S are D, so II does not follow. Answer: Both conclusions I and III follows",Hard,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2022 Tier 1 (2022-12-01 Shift 1),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2022-12-01-s1
