SSC-CGL-2022-T1-QA-EN-12562,SSC CGL,2022,Tier 1,2022-12-13,Shift 4,Quantitative Aptitude,Trigonometry & Heights,English,The table given below shows the cost price and selling price of four different items. Item Cost price Selling price J 650 750 K 370 450 L 450 650 M 590 650 What is the difference between the average selling price and the average cost price of all the items?,110,140,160,710,A,110,Average SP = (750 + 450 + 650 + 650)/4 = 2500/4 = 625. Average CP = (650 + 370 + 450 + 590)/4 = 2060/4 = 515. Difference = 625 − 515 = 110. Answer: 110,Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2022 Tier 1 (2022-12-13 Shift 4),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2022-12-13-s4
SSC-CGL-2022-T1-QA-EN-12563,SSC CGL,2022,Tier 1,2022-12-13,Shift 4,Quantitative Aptitude,Geometry,English,Find the area of the shaded part of the equilateral triangle of side 6 units shown in the figure below. The circle of radius 1 unit is centred at the midpoint of one side of the triangle.,1/2 (9√3 − 11/7) units²,1/2 (9√3 − 22/7) units²,1/4 (9√3 − 11/7) units²,1/2 (6√3 − 11/7) units²,A,1/2 (9√3 − 11/7) units²,"The altitude splits the equilateral triangle in half; the left half has area ½ × (√3/4 × 6²) = 9√3/2. The circle (radius 1) is centred at the foot of the altitude, so the part of it inside the left half is a quarter circle of area π/4 = 11/14 (π = 22/7). Shaded area = 9√3/2 − 11/14 = ½(9√3 − 11/7) square units. Answer: 1/2 (9√3 − 11/7) units²",Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2022 Tier 1 (2022-12-13 Shift 4),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2022-12-13-s4
SSC-CGL-2022-T1-QA-EN-12564,SSC CGL,2022,Tier 1,2022-12-13,Shift 4,Quantitative Aptitude,Ratio & Proportion,English,Find the mean proportional between 25 and 225.,85,65,25,75,D,75,The mean proportional between a and b is √(ab). √(25 × 225) = 5 × 15 = 75. Answer: 75,Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2022 Tier 1 (2022-12-13 Shift 4),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2022-12-13-s4
SSC-CGL-2022-T1-QA-EN-12565,SSC CGL,2022,Tier 1,2022-12-13,Shift 4,Quantitative Aptitude,Trigonometry & Heights,English,"The pie chart given below shows the number of cars sold by 8 different companies. The total number of cars sold by all these 8 companies is 5,000. The number of cars sold by a particular company is shown as a percentage of the total number of cars sold by all these 8 companies. Company Share of cars sold P 12% Q 18% R 17% S 13% T 5% U 6% V 9% W 20% (Data from the official chart, shown as a table.) What is the ratio of the total central angle made by sectors R and S to the total central angle made by sectors U and V?",3 : 1,2 : 1,1 : 4,1 : 2,B,2 : 1,Central angles are proportional to the percentages. R + S = 17% + 13% = 30%; U + V = 6% + 9% = 15%. Ratio = 30 : 15 = 2 : 1. Answer: 2 : 1,Hard,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2022 Tier 1 (2022-12-13 Shift 4),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2022-12-13-s4
SSC-CGL-2022-T1-QA-EN-12566,SSC CGL,2022,Tier 1,2022-12-13,Shift 4,Quantitative Aptitude,Arithmetic & Quantitative,English,"If a = −12, b = −6 and c = 18, then what is the value of (2abc)/9?",144,554,288,−288,C,288,abc = (−12)(−6)(18) = 1296. 2abc/9 = 2592/9 = 288. Answer: 288,Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2022 Tier 1 (2022-12-13 Shift 4),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2022-12-13-s4
SSC-CGL-2022-T1-QA-EN-12567,SSC CGL,2022,Tier 1,2022-12-13,Shift 4,Quantitative Aptitude,Trigonometry & Heights,English,A shopkeeper gives a discount of 12% to his customers and still gains a profit of 18%. Find the marked price of an article which costs the shopkeeper Rs.528. (Give your answer to the nearest rupee),Rs.728,Rs.695,Rs.798,Rs.708,D,Rs.708,SP = 528 × 118/100 = ₹623.04. SP is 88% of the marked price. Marked price = 623.04 ÷ 0.88 = ₹708. Answer: Rs.708,Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2022 Tier 1 (2022-12-13 Shift 4),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2022-12-13-s4
SSC-CGL-2022-T1-QA-EN-12568,SSC CGL,2022,Tier 1,2022-12-13,Shift 4,Quantitative Aptitude,Percentage,English,"The pie chart given below shows the number of boys in 8 schools. The total number of boys in all these 8 schools is 5000. The number of boys in a particular school is shown as a percentage of the total number of boys in all these 8 schools. School Share of boys A 26% B 4% C 5% D 25% E 13% F 10% G 9% H 8% (Data from the official chart, shown as a table.) What is the ratio of the total number of boys in B, D and E to the total number of boys in A, C and F?",37 : 57,42 : 41,57 : 38,41 : 42,B,42 : 41,"All schools share the same total, so compare percentages. B + D + E = 4 + 25 + 13 = 42%; A + C + F = 26 + 5 + 10 = 41%. Ratio = 42 : 41. Answer: 42 : 41",Hard,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2022 Tier 1 (2022-12-13 Shift 4),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2022-12-13-s4
SSC-CGL-2022-T1-QA-EN-12569,SSC CGL,2022,Tier 1,2022-12-13,Shift 4,Quantitative Aptitude,"Profit, Loss & Discount",English,"By selling an article for Rs.33,000, a person makes a profit of 10%. At what price should he sell it to make a profit of 20%?","Rs.30,000","Rs.35,000","Rs.36,000","Rs.36,600",C,"Rs.36,000","CP = 33000 ÷ 1.10 = ₹30,000. For a 20% profit, SP = 30000 × 1.20 = ₹36,000. Answer: Rs.36,000",Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2022 Tier 1 (2022-12-13 Shift 4),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2022-12-13-s4
SSC-CGL-2022-T1-QA-EN-12570,SSC CGL,2022,Tier 1,2022-12-13,Shift 4,Quantitative Aptitude,Average & Statistics,English,A batsman has an average of 58 runs in nine innings of cricket. How many runs must he score in the tenth innings to get an average score of 61?,69,79,100,88,D,88,Runs in 9 innings = 9 × 58 = 522. For an average of 61 over 10 innings he needs 610 runs. Runs needed in the 10th innings = 610 − 522 = 88. Answer: 88,Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2022 Tier 1 (2022-12-13 Shift 4),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2022-12-13-s4
SSC-CGL-2022-T1-QA-EN-12571,SSC CGL,2022,Tier 1,2022-12-13,Shift 4,Quantitative Aptitude,Trigonometry & Heights,English,"If tan(α + β) = a and tan(α − β) = b, then find the value of tan 2α.",(a + b)/(1 + ab),(a − b)/(1 − ab),(a − b)/(1 + ab),(a + b)/(1 − ab),D,(a + b)/(1 − ab),"2α = (α + β) + (α − β), so tan 2α = [tan(α + β) + tan(α − β)] / [1 − tan(α + β) tan(α − β)]. tan 2α = (a + b)/(1 − ab). Answer: (a + b)/(1 − ab)",Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2022 Tier 1 (2022-12-13 Shift 4),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2022-12-13-s4
SSC-CGL-2022-T1-QA-EN-12572,SSC CGL,2022,Tier 1,2022-12-13,Shift 4,Quantitative Aptitude,"Profit, Loss & Discount",English,Select the most appropriate option to complete the given sentence. They were not able to get the item from the store because it was __________.,in vain,out of stock,on leave,in fashion,B,out of stock,"“Out of stock” means not available in the store, which explains why the item could not be bought. “In vain” means without success, “on leave” is used for people, and “in fashion” does not fit. Answer: out of stock",Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2022 Tier 1 (2022-12-13 Shift 4),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2022-12-13-s4
SSC-CGL-2022-T1-QA-EN-12573,SSC CGL,2022,Tier 1,2022-12-13,Shift 4,Quantitative Aptitude,"Profit, Loss & Discount",English,"In the given sentence, one word may have been incorrectly spelt. Select the correctly spelt word from the given alternatives. If there is no error, select ‘No error’ as your answer. Decades of enimity between Lomovs and Chubukovs came to an end with the marriage between the two families.",enmity,enemity,No error,anaemity,A,enmity,The correct spelling is “enmity” (hostility). Answer: enmity,Hard,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2022 Tier 1 (2022-12-13 Shift 4),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2022-12-13-s4
SSC-CGL-2022-T1-QA-EN-12574,SSC CGL,2022,Tier 1,2022-12-13,Shift 4,Quantitative Aptitude,Arithmetic & Quantitative,English,Select the most appropriate ANTONYM of the given word. Inherent,Natural,Induce,extraneous,Restricted,C,extraneous,"Inherent means existing naturally as an essential part; extraneous means coming from outside, not essential — its opposite. Answer: extraneous",Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2022 Tier 1 (2022-12-13 Shift 4),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2022-12-13-s4
SSC-CGL-2022-T1-QA-EN-12575,SSC CGL,2022,Tier 1,2022-12-13,Shift 4,Quantitative Aptitude,"Time, Work & Pipes",English,"Select the most appropriate option that can substitute the underlined segment in the given sentence. If there is no need to substitute it, select ‘No substitution’. Kranthi loves to swim at the local swimming pool in order that relieve his work stress.",No substitution,in order which,in order as to relieve,in order to relieve,D,in order to relieve,Purpose is expressed with “in order to” + base verb: “in order to relieve his work stress”. Answer: in order to relieve,Hard,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2022 Tier 1 (2022-12-13 Shift 4),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2022-12-13-s4
SSC-CGL-2022-T1-QA-EN-12576,SSC CGL,2022,Tier 1,2022-12-13,Shift 4,Quantitative Aptitude,Simple & Compound Interest,English,Select the most appropriate ANTONYM of the word ‘dwindle’ in the sentence given below. Rahul advised Neerja to appreciate or motivate her younger brother and not to dwindle or neglect his ideas all the time.,deprecate,motivate,complement,neglect,C,complement,"In the sentence, “dwindle” is used in the sense of belittling or running down (paired with “neglect”). SSC’s answer is “complement”, intended as the opposite sense (praise/add to). “Deprecate” and “neglect” are near-synonyms in this context. Answer: complement",Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2022 Tier 1 (2022-12-13 Shift 4),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2022-12-13-s4
