SSC-CGL-2024-T1-QA-EN-14498,SSC CGL,2024,Tier 1,2024-09-09,Shift 1,Quantitative Aptitude,Percentage,English,"In an election between two candidates, y% of the voters did not vote. 10% of the votes cast were declared invalid, while all the valid votes were cast in favour of either of the two candidates. The candidate who got 59.375% of the valid votes cast was declared elected by 2484 votes. If the number of people eligible to vote in that election was 16,000, what is the value of y?",8,8.4,7.5,7.2,A,8,"Option (1) is correct. Total no. of eligible voters = 16000 No, of people vote = (100 – y)% = 16000 = (100 – y) × 160 = 16000 – 160y Number of valid votes = 90 100 16000 160 × − ( ) y According to questions, ( . . )% ( ) 59 375 40 625 90 100 16000 160 − −       of y =2484 ⇒ 18 75 100 90 100 . × (16000 – 160y) = 2484 ⇒ y = 8",Hard,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2024 Tier 1 (2024-09-09 Shift 1) | 2024 Tier 1 (2024-09-09 Shift 1 (Morning)),https://www.oswaal360.com/pluginfile.php/10939/mod_folder/content/0/SSC/SSC%20CGL/2024/9%20Sep%202024%20S1.pdf
SSC-CGL-2024-T1-QA-EN-14499,SSC CGL,2024,Tier 1,2024-09-09,Shift 1,Quantitative Aptitude,Trigonometry & Heights,English,"An article is marked at ` 550. If it is sold at a discount of 40%, then the selling price becomes 10% more than its cost price. What is the cost price (in `)?",330,220,300,"200 64. If the sum of two sides of an equilateral triangle is 16 cm, then find the third side. 1. Cannot be found 2. 16 cm 3. 4 cm 4. 8 cm 65. Simplify: x x 2 9 3 − ( ) + 1. x – 9 2. x + 3 3. x – 3 4. x2 9 −",C,300,"Option (3) is correct. M.P = ` 550 S.P = (100 – 40)% of ` 550 = 60 100 550 × = ` 330 So, S.P = 110% of C.P 330 = 110 100 × C.P ⇒ C.P = ` 300",Hard,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2024 Tier 1 (2024-09-09 Shift 1),https://www.oswaal360.com/pluginfile.php/10939/mod_folder/content/0/SSC/SSC%20CGL/2024/9%20Sep%202024%20S1.pdf
SSC-CGL-2024-T1-QA-EN-14500,SSC CGL,2024,Tier 1,2024-09-09,Shift 1,Quantitative Aptitude,Trigonometry & Heights,English,"The following table shows the total candidates appeared and number of candidates present, in different exam centres – P, Q and R. Study the table and answer the question that follows. Year P Q R Total Present Total Present Total Present 2016 45 42 56 45 55 50 2017 50 45 75 55 45 40 2018 64 60 65 45 65 55 2019 80 69 84 72 70 62 2020 55 44 80 66 90 85 ‘Total’ denotes total candidates applied for the centre,‘Present’ denotes the candidates appeared. In which year was the number of absentees the second highest in total across all centres?",2018,2019,2020,2017,B,2019,Option (2) is correct. Number of absentees in 2016 = (45 – 42) + (56 – 45) + (55 – 50) = 3 + 11 + 5 = 19 Number of absentees in 2017 = (50 – 45) + (75 – 55) + (45 – 40) = 5 + 20 + 5 = 30 Number of absentees in 2018 = (64 – 60) + (65 – 45) + (65 – 55) = 4 + 20 + 10 = 34 Number of absentees in 2019 = (80 – 69) + (84 – 72) + (70 – 62) = 11 + 12 + 8 = 31 Number of absentees in 2020 = (55 – 44) + (80 – 66) + (90 – 85) = 11 + 14 + 5 = 30,Hard,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2024 Tier 1 (2024-09-09 Shift 1),https://www.oswaal360.com/pluginfile.php/10939/mod_folder/content/0/SSC/SSC%20CGL/2024/9%20Sep%202024%20S1.pdf
