SSC-CGL-2024-T1-GI-EN-15647,SSC CGL,2024,Tier 1,2024-09-11,Shift 2,General Intelligence & Reasoning,Blood Relations,English,"Select the triad in which the numbers are related to each other in the same way as the numbers in the following triads. 8-17-35 23-47-95 (NOTE : Operations should be performed on the whole numbers, without breaking down the numbers into its constituent digits. E.g. 13 – Operations on 13 such as adding /deleting /multiplying etc., to 13 can be performed. Breaking down 13 into 1 and 3 and then performing mathematical operations on 1 and 3 is not allowed.)",12-25-51,9-20-41,13-29-55,16-32-62,A,12-25-51,"In each triad, every number is twice the previous number plus 1: 8×2+1=17, 17×2+1=35; 23×2+1=47, 47×2+1=95. Check: 12×2+1=25 and 25×2+1=51. The other triads break the rule (e.g. 9×2+1=19, not 20). Answer: 12-25-51",Hard,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2024 Tier 1 (2024-09-11 Shift 2) | 2024 Tier 1 (2024-09-11 Shift 2 (Afternoon)),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2024-09-11-s2
SSC-CGL-2024-T1-GI-EN-15648,SSC CGL,2024,Tier 1,2024-09-11,Shift 2,General Intelligence & Reasoning,Blood Relations,English,"In a certain code language, A + B means ‘A is the wife of B’. A − B means ‘A is the son of B’. A × B means ‘A is the husband of B’. A ÷ B means ‘A is the father of B’. Based on the above, how is P related to T if ‘P − Q + R ÷ S × T’?",Father-in-law,Husband’s brother,Son’s son,Father,B,Husband’s brother,"P − Q: P is the son of Q. Q + R: Q is the wife of R, so R is P's father. R ÷ S: R is the father of S, so S is P's brother. S × T: S is the husband of T. So P is the brother of T's husband. Answer: Husband’s brother",Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2024 Tier 1 (2024-09-11 Shift 2) | 2024 Tier 1 (2024-09-11 Shift 2 (Afternoon)),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2024-09-11-s2
SSC-CGL-2024-T1-GI-EN-15649,SSC CGL,2024,Tier 1,2024-09-11,Shift 2,General Intelligence & Reasoning,Blood Relations,English,‘Lavish’ is related to ‘Austere’ in the same way as ‘Prejudiced’ is related to ‘________’. (The words must be considered as meaningful English words and must not be related to each other based on the number of letters/number of consonants/vowels in the word.),Compromised,Careful,Thoughtful,Impartial,D,Impartial,"Lavish (extravagant) and austere (plain, simple) are opposites. The opposite of prejudiced (biased) is impartial (fair, unbiased). Answer: Impartial",Medium,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2024 Tier 1 (2024-09-11 Shift 2) | 2024 Tier 1 (2024-09-11 Shift 2 (Afternoon)),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2024-09-11-s2
SSC-CGL-2024-T1-GI-EN-15650,SSC CGL,2024,Tier 1,2024-09-11,Shift 2,General Intelligence & Reasoning,Syllogism & Statements,English,Read the given statements and conclusions carefully. You have to take the given statements to be true even if they seem to be at variance from commonly known facts. You have to decide which conclusion/s logically follow/s from the given statements. Statements: All apples are mangoes. All mangoes are lemons. All lemons are kiwis. Conclusions: (I) All apples are lemons. (II) All mangoes are kiwis.,Both conclusions I and II follow.,Only conclusion I follows.,Neither conclusion I nor II follow.,Only conclusion II follows.,A,Both conclusions I and II follow.,Apples ⊂ mangoes ⊂ lemons ⊂ kiwis. So every apple is a lemon (I follows) and every mango is a kiwi (II follows). Answer: Both conclusions I and II follow.,Hard,Verified,Verified (Archived Official Key),2024 Tier 1 (2024-09-11 Shift 2) | 2024 Tier 1 (2024-09-11 Shift 2 (Afternoon)),https://www.nextgenpsc.com/ssc/cgl/previous-year-papers/cgl-2024-09-11-s2
