SSC-CGL-2025-T1-EN-EN-20660,SSC CGL,2025,Tier 1,2025-09-13,Shift 2,English Comprehension,English Grammar & Vocabulary,English,Change the following from active to passive: We shall have dispatched the invitations before Friday evening.,The invitations shall have dispatched before Friday evening.,The invitations shall have had been dispatched before Friday evening.,The invitations shall have be dispatched before Friday evening.,The invitations shall have been dispatched before Friday evening.,D,The invitations shall have been dispatched before Friday evening.,The future perfect passive is shall have been + past participle: “The invitations shall have been dispatched before Friday evening.”,Hard,Verified,Verified (Official Response Sheet Key),2025 Tier 1 (2025-09-13 Shift 2),https://www.bookofexams.in/pyp/ssc-cgl/ssc-cgl-2025-tier-1-question-paper-13-september-shift-2/
SSC-CGL-2025-T1-EN-EN-20661,SSC CGL,2025,Tier 1,2025-09-13,Shift 2,English Comprehension,English Grammar & Vocabulary,English,Select the sentence containing the homonym of the highlighted word: The medieval ‘oriel’ window projected elegantly from the chapel wall. (Highlighted word shown in quotes.),The priest admired the sunlight filtering through the oriel.,The duke stood by the oriel gazing at the courtyard.,The technician adjusted the frequency on the oriel.,The stained-glass oriel was restored by artisans.,C,The technician adjusted the frequency on the oriel.,"SSC keyed C. “Oriel” has one meaning, a projecting bay window, so no option has a true homonym. Option C is the only sentence where the word cannot mean a window, which is how the key reads it. Arguable question. The answer is kept as SSC keyed it.",Hard,Verified,Verified (Official Response Sheet Key),2025 Tier 1 (2025-09-13 Shift 2),https://www.bookofexams.in/pyp/ssc-cgl/ssc-cgl-2025-tier-1-question-paper-13-september-shift-2/
